A shopping agent wants to end without buying because it thinks nothing fits. Check the listing records shown against the requirements, with these reading rules: a base or boost clock or frequency (keys such as base clock, base frequency, boost clock) does not state a processor speed or clock speed requirement, and neither does a GHz figure in the title or description of a listing whose attributes give only such a base or boost value; the listing's attribute for a property decides when its key names that same property (any key spelling, one element of a list is enough, synonyms and unit conversions count, a value containing the required value's words counts, such as "deep teal" for teal, limits include the edge); a value under a key that adds the item's own name to the property (a bag's "bag shape") counts even when the bare key ("shape") says something else; a key for a different property, even a related one (a handle colour is not the body's colour), decides nothing; when no attribute names that property, the wanted value stated under another attribute or in the title or description counts (a bare figure that does not say what it measures counts for nothing); a requirement the category label itself states is met by that category; the category label must match the item; a rule of being unlike another listing's value counts as met unless the records show that same value; out-of-stock listings and listings that break the budget do not fit.
Records marked ROW were seen only as a search result row: their page (the attributes) has not been read, so they can be neither passed nor failed on a property the row does not show.
Return ONE JSON object and nothing else: {"fits": {"item number": [listing ids that appear to meet every requirement, cheapest first, at most 4]}, "open": [ids of ROW listings of a requested kind, inside the budget, whose page could show that they fit, most promising first, at most 6], "reason": "one sentence"}

REQUEST:
